{
46: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated factor that affects seed germination.", question: "According to the second paragraph, which factor may influence whether a moistened seed leaves dormancy?", option1: "The color of nearby flowers", option2: "The number of mature plants visible above it", option3: "The direction from which rain approaches", option4: "The length of time the seed remains wet", correctAnswer: "The length of time the seed remains wet", explanation: "Step 1: The paragraph states that absorbing water begins germination but may not by itself release dormancy.<br><br>Step 2: It explicitly lists the length of wetting among the additional influences.<br><br>Answer: The length of time the seed remains wet", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
47: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a scientific qualifier.", question: "As used in the seventh paragraph, the word 'universally' most nearly means:", option1: "under all environmental conditions.", option2: "throughout one growing season.", option3: "among seeds from one parent plant.", option4: "according to every researcher.", correctAnswer: "under all environmental conditions.", explanation: "Step 1: The paragraph contrasts dependable rainfall, erratic rainfall, and different locations within one desert.<br><br>Step 2: A strategy that succeeds in one setting may not succeed in another, so 'universally' means under all environmental conditions.<br><br>Answer: under all environmental conditions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
48: {skill_tested: "Identify the central idea of a scientific explanation.", question: "Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", option1: "Desert annuals survive primarily because all their seeds germinate immediately after rain.", option2: "Variation in germination timing distributes risk across uncertain conditions, though every timing strategy has costs.", option3: "Seeds buried at greater depths always remain viable longer than seeds near the surface.", option4: "Researchers can determine the best germination strategy by counting seedlings after one storm.", correctAnswer: "Variation in germination timing distributes risk across uncertain conditions, though every timing strategy has costs.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage describes several schedules produced by dormancy controls and variation among seeds.<br><br>Step 2: It repeatedly connects those schedules to benefits and risks under unpredictable rainfall.<br><br>Answer: Variation in germination timing distributes risk across uncertain conditions, though every timing strategy has costs.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
49: {skill_tested: "Explain the relationship between a mechanism and its ecological effect.", question: "The controls described in the second paragraph help a seed population avoid:", option1: "producing seeds with different coat thicknesses on the same plant.", option2: "being distributed among shallow soil, drainage channels, and rocky slopes.", option3: "having every seed respond to a brief rain as though it guarantees a complete season.", option4: "requiring water at any point during germination.", correctAnswer: "having every seed respond to a brief rain as though it guarantees a complete season.", explanation: "Step 1: Moisture, temperature, wetting duration, seed coats, and parental conditions alter the release of dormancy.<br><br>Step 2: These controls cause neighboring seeds to follow different schedules.<br><br>Step 3: The resulting variation prevents one possibly misleading shower from triggering the whole population.<br><br>Answer: having every seed respond to a brief rain as though it guarantees a complete season.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
50: {skill_tested: "Determine how a paragraph qualifies an appealing analogy.", question: "The fourth paragraph primarily qualifies the comparison between a seed bank and distributing risk by explaining that:", option1: "dormant seeds avoid every danger faced by growing plants.", option2: "seed banks occur only where rainfall is relatively dependable.", option3: "all seeds in a bank eventually germinate during the same season.", option4: "reducing the danger of one failed season exposes waiting seeds to other dangers.", correctAnswer: "reducing the danger of one failed season exposes waiting seeds to other dangers.", explanation: "Step 1: Germinating only some seeds protects the population from losing every offspring in one failed season.<br><br>Step 2: The paragraph then lists predation, burial, infection, and displacement as costs of remaining dormant.<br><br>Step 3: The analogy is therefore qualified as an exchange among risks, not the removal of risk.<br><br>Answer: reducing the danger of one failed season exposes waiting seeds to other dangers.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
51: {skill_tested: "Analyze why complementary observations are required in an experiment.", question: "Why does the passage indicate that researchers use both buried mesh packets and nearby plots?", option1: "The packets reveal below-ground seed outcomes while the plots reveal which schedules lead to mature plants.", option2: "The packets measure rainfall while the plots measure soil temperature.", option3: "The packets contain rapid seeds while the plots contain only dormant seeds.", option4: "The two methods ensure that every seed is observed without being disturbed.", correctAnswer: "The packets reveal below-ground seed outcomes while the plots reveal which schedules lead to mature plants.", explanation: "Step 1: Retrieved packets distinguish dormant, germinated, and damaged seeds below ground.<br><br>Step 2: Plot counts follow naturally emerging seedlings through maturity.<br><br>Step 3: A strategy can look successful by one measure and fail by the other, so the methods answer complementary parts of the question.<br><br>Answer: The packets reveal below-ground seed outcomes while the plots reveal which schedules lead to mature plants.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
52: {skill_tested: "Infer the value of variation within one plant's offspring.", question: "The sixth paragraph most strongly suggests that differences among seeds from one parent can benefit the population because the differences:", option1: "allow the parent plant to predict the exact date of future rain.", option2: "create several possible responses before future conditions are known.", option3: "ensure that all offspring receive identical environmental signals.", option4: "prevent seeds from developing thick coats in shaded locations.", correctAnswer: "create several possible responses before future conditions are known.", explanation: "Step 1: Developmental conditions can produce seeds that respond differently even when they share a parent.<br><br>Step 2: The parent need not sense or predict the future for this variation to occur.<br><br>Step 3: Because several responses already exist when the next season arrives, at least some may suit conditions the parent could not foresee.<br><br>Answer: create several possible responses before future conditions are known.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
53: {skill_tested: "Synthesize experimental and environmental evidence to evaluate success.", question: "Which finding would provide the strongest evidence that a persistent seed-bank strategy benefits a population over several years?", option1: "After one shower, most rapid-germinating seedlings emerge before seed-bank seedlings.", option2: "Dormant seeds are found at several depths after a season with dependable rainfall.", option3: "Across alternating failed and favorable seasons, some stored seeds survive to germinate and replenish the bank after reproducing.", option4: "Seeds in mesh packets absorb water at the same time as seeds in nearby plots.", correctAnswer: "Across alternating failed and favorable seasons, some stored seeds survive to germinate and replenish the bank after reproducing.", explanation: "Step 1: A seed bank is valuable because it retains opportunities when a particular above-ground season fails.<br><br>Step 2: Its dormant seeds also face losses, and eventual germination alone is insufficient unless plants reproduce.<br><br>Step 3: Evidence across both failed and favorable years that links survival, later germination, reproduction, and renewed storage demonstrates the complete proposed benefit.<br><br>Answer: Across alternating failed and favorable seasons, some stored seeds survive to germinate and replenish the bank after reproducing.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
54: {skill_tested: "Interpret the conclusion by integrating mechanism, variation, and visibility.", question: "The final statement that many possibilities remain beneath the visible plants most nearly emphasizes that:", option1: "researchers cannot learn anything about seeds without removing all soil.", option2: "every dormant seed will become visible during the following season.", option3: "above-ground plants are less important than seeds under all conditions.", option4: "the plants that emerge represent only some of the population's varied responses to uncertainty.", correctAnswer: "the plants that emerge represent only some of the population's varied responses to uncertainty.", explanation: "Step 1: Earlier paragraphs establish that controls and developmental variation prevent all seeds from germinating together.<br><br>Step 2: The visible plants are therefore the subset whose schedules produced current growth, not the entire living population.<br><br>Step 3: Calling dormant seeds possibilities connects hidden variation with future seasons whose conditions remain unknown.<br><br>Answer: the plants that emerge represent only some of the population's varied responses to uncertainty.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
